Multiplayer nethack, the topic that periodically reappears before returning
to hibernation. I'll quote some of my thoughts I wrote in rec.games.programmer.
(Somewhat changed since the original posting.)

How to time multiplayer nethack, "attunement". This is a method of interaction
between players that preserves as much original 'hack' feeling as possible
without restricting the speed in which the players play.
(characters in this soap opera: players A,B; monster M.)

 Monster M is attuned to exactly one player at a time. Its attunement change
takes one of its own moves (reason explained later). When the player moves,
monster gets one chance of moving (when player and monster are equally fast.)
When player is slower, monster gets two chances and when the monster is slower
it gets a chance of movement every two player moves.

 M is usually attuned to the closest player, or, when two players are close
enough, to the player that it is fighting against.

 This has the following advantages: when you fight against monsters YOU
decide how fast you want to play; when you do nothing neither do the monsters.
You can also decide how fast you want to play, it is not imposed on you by
the game or by other players.

 Of course the other player may choose to attack you at any time. Here comes
into picture 'automatic defense'. When he hits you, you automatically defend
yourself. The defense does NOT continue automatically. When he hits you,
you automatically hit back. When he stops, you stop. Automatic defense does
NOT take any of your 'ordinary' time. In addition to automatic defense, you
may hit him normally. Then he in turn automatically defends.

 Note that when a player does nothing he can be hit ONLY by another player.
The monsters that are attacking him wait for him to move. So, the attacking
player is hit by monsters and automatic defense, but the defending player
only by the attacking player. This favors the defending player, as it should.

I mentioned a reason why attunement change takes one of monster's moves. 
Consider the situation in which two players are attacking a single monster.
    
     A <-> M <-> B

According to the rules mentioned the monster's attunement should change to
the player that hit it last. Suppose all the characters are equally fast.
If A hits then M's attunement changes to it and M gets a chance to hit A.
Then B hits M and after attunement change M gets a chance to hit B. Continuing
in this fashion M gets to hit twice as often as the players, but they WERE
equally fast... This is solved by requiring one move for attunement change:
A hits M, M changes attunement. (Red dragon turns to confront the other player)
B hits M, M changes attunement.
The monster currently seems confused who to hit and doesn't get anything done.
Because a monster should fight, after all, it has some chance of doing the
attunement change and attacking in a single move (depending on the number
of players it is confronting.)

When a player starts wearing armor that takes, say, ten moves he does so at
some constant speed, but if there is another player near that is moving faster
the this constant speed, then the wearing happens as fast as the other player
moves. 

>The system will also have to have alot more food (and other stuff) 
>to support not only the warriors, but their little pets.

This seems obvious. Normally a level would have enough stuff to support
a single player (and keep him busy;-), but when additional players enter
the level there is additional generation of monsters and items, until
there is enough for them all. When the additional players leave, there
begins a reduction in the number of items (a hobgoblin picks something and
leaves by mysterious ways known to no man.)

>Which leads onto the next point, what happens when the pet dies?
>Will the gods be slightly angry with one character or the whole group.
>What happens when a god is totally pissed off with his character?
[Etc. Etc.]

In my theme a group is made up of individual players that move themselves.
So, everyone is responsible for his own actions. If your god gets pissed
off with you, you suffer. No-one else. Also if player A's dog wants to attack
player B's cat, it's there business. No-one else's.

So, what actually is a group of players? Just individual players that have
decided to stick close together. From the game's point of view there is 
almost no difference when comparing N individual players and a group of 
N players. The one difference is that "group spells" work on those players
that stayed together for an extended period of time.

>>> Since players can work as teams, why not monsters of the dungeon. 
>>>In all fairness this should probably also be included. For instance,
>>>dwarfs combine with goblins to form parties and actually work as parties
>>>instead of just individuals as they seem to do in normal hack.

They try to do that now. Only, it's so difficult to program (or at least
time consuming.) There shouldn't be any reason not to put in soldiers that
obey their sergeant's orders.

>If everyone is affected by a magic spell casted by one character, then
>what happens when he/she casts something like a teleport spell and
>everyone is scattered over the dungeon. Does everyone teleport. No,
>I don't think so. Perhaps what could be done, is have some spells
>where the effects are felt by everyone (within a certain distance
>or room) and others that only affect the caster (personally, an
>enchant weapon spell, I would only like to use on my weapon
>and not everyone else, since weapons do explode after several
>enchantments). A side effect of this is that there a various ranges of
>spells. For instance, you can get a magic mapping spell, which only you
>can see and a magic mapping spell which everyone within a range can see.

The way to do it is that normally all magic works, well, normally..
That magic mapping scroll gives you information that you may decide to tell
others or you may decide otherwise. If you decide to tell, they get the whole
map (possibly for a fee). There could be some special spells though, like
"a scroll of group teleport".

>For example, does a ring of conflict affect everyone apart from
>the wearer or does it affect everyone besides the group? If the
>first case, do they fight among themselves or what?

A ring of conflict affects only those monster that are "attuned" to the player
wearing the ring. Otherwise the group would become too powerful.

>What about a ring of invisibility? Is the wearer invisible to
>everyone or just those outside the group?

I should say he/she is invisible to everyone. This way you could do some
nice jokes to other players in your group (like picking someone else's
pockets.)

>What happens with empowered corpses? If one person eats a dead eye,
>does everyone see monsters or just that person. I would imagine it
>is only the eater. What happens if two people share in eating a
>dead leprachaun, do both start teleporting about or neither.

Two people can't share a leprechaun, there is only enough food for one!

As a final comment I want to add that I don't think Nethack can be converted
to a multiplayer game without a complete rewrite. Prove me wrong!
